  "worked_solution": "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+x-6,$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,x$ | $-$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+x-6\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\,\\,}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | $+$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $6x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | $-$ | ${x}^{2}$ | $-$ | $x$ | $+$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $7x$ | $-$ | $6$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x-1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-6I) \\\\  & =7A-6I.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
